-- Algebraic typeclasses — the canonical FP trinity declared as ordinary
-- Fenel spec-record types (Phase 5 of typeclass-substrate-completion,
-- 2026-05-29). This is the payoff of the workstream: Functor / Monad /
-- Monoid are ordinary record types, their Maybe / List / Result instances
-- are ordinary typed values (fenel/std/algebra-instances.fenel), and
-- `fmap` / `bind` (`>>=`) / `mappend` (`<>`) dispatch by use-site reflection
-- through the same general surface (design/unified-dispatch.md) every other
-- typeclass / effect / capability uses — no `Monad` keyword, no per-class
-- engine branch.
--
-- HIGHER-KINDED APPLICATION + the dispatch seed. The dispatcher
-- (effect-dispatch.ts `dispatchPositionForSeed`) reads each method's
-- declared param types to derive WHICH argument position carries the class
-- type-parameter — `bind : m a -> …` dispatches on arg 0 because the class
-- param `m` heads arg 0. That derivation requires the param type to be a
-- type-application headed by the class var (`{$el:{call:[{$var:"m"},…]}}`),
-- whose authoritative scheme sidecar now carries `m` as a kinded constructor
-- variable. These class-record method fields retain angle application `m<a>`
-- to keep the arrow-field/sibling boundary byte-stable. The DISPATCH-AUTHORITATIVE class
-- seeds are the TS `SpecRecordSeed` literals in `engine/src/builtin-classes.ts`
-- (`functorClass` / `monadClass` / `monoidClass`), mirroring the synthetic
-- seeds the Phase 3.5 oracle pins. THIS file is the model / catalog surface:
-- it declares the same three classes as readable Fenel types (using angle-
-- bracket application `f<a>` so each method parses to a single record field),
-- so the catalog surfaces them in the "spec" section like every other class.
-- The two coexist exactly as every representational class does (a TS seed in
-- builtin-classes.ts + a .fenel spec type in typeclass-specs.fenel).
--
-- `return` (Monad) and `mempty` (Monoid) are DECLARED — the types are
-- complete — but they are RETURN-POSITION methods (the class param appears in
-- no argument), so there is nothing to reflect on: they surface a
-- None-with-reason at dispatch and are inference-owned (out of scope this
-- phase). `fmap` / `bind` / `mappend` are argument-position and dispatch live.

Functor f = Functor fmap: (a -> b) -> f<a> -> f<b>

Monad m = Monad bind: m<a> -> (a -> m<b>) -> m<b> return: a -> m<a>

Monoid a = Monoid mappend: a -> a -> a mempty: a

-- Foldable — `foldr : (a -> b -> b) -> b -> f a -> b`. The right-fold over a
-- structure's elements (unified-static-analysis Phase 6c). The class param `f`
-- heads the THIRD argument (`f<a>`, after the binop + the seed), so dispatch
-- keys on arg position 2 — derived from this `.fenel` decl by
-- `dispatchPositionForSeed` (the same `f<a>` angle-bracket shape `fmap` uses,
-- so NO `foldableClass` TS dispatch-seed literal is needed; D-6c-1). `foldr` is
-- the PRIMITIVE; `toList` / `length` / `sum` derive from it as ordinary std
-- functions over `Foldable` (algebra-methods.fenel). Like the trinity, the
-- dispatch seed is DERIVED from this record by `seedFromType` (system-entities.ts
-- adds "Foldable"→"f" to the trinity loop). The nominal `Maybe`/`List`/`Result`
-- instances (algebra-instances.fenel) keep precedence; a single always-on
-- structural instance (`engine:deriveFoldr`) gives every other `* -> *` type
-- `foldr` for FREE (the Functor-derivation precedent).
Foldable f = Foldable foldr: (a -> b -> b) -> b -> f<a> -> b

-- Applicative — the standard rung between Functor and Monad
-- (unified-static-analysis Phase 6d). `pure : a -> f<a>` lifts a value into the
-- applicative; `ap : f<(a -> b)> -> f<a> -> f<b>` (`<*>`) applies a wrapped
-- function to a wrapped argument. The class param `f` heads the FIRST argument
-- of `ap` (`f<(a -> b)>`), so dispatch keys on arg position 0 — derived from this
-- `.fenel` decl by `dispatchPositionForSeed` (the `f<…>` angle-bracket shape
-- `fmap`/`foldr` use, so NO `applicativeClass` TS dispatch-seed literal is needed;
-- D-6d-1). Like the trinity, the dispatch seed is DERIVED from this record by
-- `seedFromType` (system-entities.ts adds "Applicative"→"f" to the loop).
--
-- `ap` is the dispatchable, argument-position method (probe-confirmed position 0).
-- `pure` is RETURN-POSITION (`f` appears only in the return — exactly like
-- `Monad.return` / `Monoid.mempty`): declared on the class for completeness, not
-- dispatched on a value, inference-owned. It rides on each instance as a constant
-- handle (the `mempty` precedent) so `Traversable.traverse` can resolve `pure`
-- from the applicative `g` (Phase 6d threads it from the registry, not a runtime
-- value — see derive-traversable.fenel).
--
-- DERIVED Applicative is FENCED (D-6d-1): `pure` is return-position (can't
-- dispatch on a value) and a generic structural `ap` over an arbitrary schema is
-- ill-defined (which fields combine how?). Only the nominal `Maybe`/`List`/
-- `Result` instances (algebra-instances.fenel) are authored.
Applicative f = Applicative pure: a -> f<a> ap: f<(a -> b)> -> f<a> -> f<b>

-- Traversable — `traverse : (a -> g<b>) -> f<a> -> g<f<b> >` (unified-static-
-- analysis Phase 6d). Walks a structure `f<a>` applying an effectful function
-- `a -> g<b>` (`g` an `Applicative`), SEQUENCING the effects, producing
-- `g<f<b> >`. `sequenceA = traverse id` derives from it as an ordinary std fn
-- over `Traversable` (algebra-methods.fenel, the `toList`/`foldableLength`
-- precedent). The class param `f` heads the SECOND argument (`f<a>`; arg 0 is the
-- effectful function `(a -> g<b>)`), so dispatch keys on arg position 1 —
-- probe-confirmed off this derived seed (the nested arrow param `(a -> g<b>)` is
-- a single field, NOT flattened into the method spine, so position counting is
-- correct). NO `traversableClass` TS literal (D-6d-1, `.fenel`-derived). The
-- return `g<f<b> >` carries a SPACE before the final `>` so the lexer does not
-- read `>>` as a right-shift token (it is return-position, dispatch-irrelevant).
--
-- THE EMPTY-APPLICATIVE resolution (D-6d-2): in the empty/leaf case `traverse`
-- must return `pure (empty structure)` in `g`, but `g` is determined by the
-- RETURN type of `(a -> g<b>)`, NOT by a runtime value (Fenel dispatches on
-- values; Haskell resolves this by TYPES). Phase 6d resolves `g` in the
-- category-B `engine:deriveTraverse` bridge — from the effectful function's
-- declared return type (the SOUND path, works for the empty case) with a
-- non-empty first-element reflection fallback — and THREADS the resolved
-- `Applicative g`'s `pure`/`ap` handles into the `.fenel` decision body. So the
-- body never guesses `g`; an unresolvable all-empty-over-an-opaque-closure case
-- is the documented fence, never a silently-wrong applicative.
Traversable f = Traversable traverse: (a -> g<b>) -> f<a> -> g<f<b> >

-- Representable — the array-flavored class (array-substrate Phase 1b). A functor
-- `f` is REPRESENTABLE when `f a ≅ (i -> a)` for some index type `i`: the
-- container IS a TOTAL function from its index set to its values (Gibbons'
-- Naperian-functor insight; Dex's `n => a`). This is exactly what an array is —
-- `Vec n a = $map (Fin n) a` is a total function `Fin n → a`. Two methods, the
-- two halves of the isomorphism:
--   • `index : f<a> -> i -> a` — look up the value at an index. The class param
--     `f` heads the FIRST argument (`f<a>`), so dispatch keys on arg position 0
--     — derived from this `.fenel` decl by `dispatchPositionForSeed` (the `fmap`
--     `f<a>` shape; NO TS dispatch-seed literal, `.fenel`-derived, the Foldable/
--     Applicative precedent). For `Vec` it is TOTAL (`i : Fin n` is in-bounds by
--     construction — D3): the body peels the partial `std:mapGet` Maybe under
--     the documented in-domain assumption.
--   • `tabulate : (i -> a) -> f<a>` — BUILD the container from an index function
--     (apply it at every index). This is RETURN-POSITION (`f` appears only in the
--     return, exactly like `Applicative.pure` / `Monad.return` / `Monoid.mempty`):
--     it cannot be dispatched on a value (Fenel dispatches on values; there is no
--     `f`-typed argument to reflect), AND a bare index-fn value carries no SHAPE
--     (a `Vec`'s `n`). So it is DECLARED on the class for completeness + the
--     isomorphism story, but the VALUE-LEVEL builder is the standalone
--     `tabulateVec : (n: Number) -> (i -> a) -> f<a>` (algebra-methods.fenel),
--     which takes `n` explicitly (`tabulateVec n g = map g [0..n-1]`). Hence the
--     `Representable Vec` instance authors ONLY `index` (tabulate omitted — the
--     `Monad.return` precedent).
--
-- THE SCALAR is a CONSTANT FUNCTOR (array-substrate Phase 4, Q2 — the "third way",
-- design § 9 / notes/phase-4-prototype.md). A rank-0 scalar (`Array [] a = a`, the
-- bare element) participates in `index` WITHOUT a runtime wrapper: `index scalar _
-- = scalar` (the constant function — looking up ANY index returns the scalar). This
-- is the operational core of broadcasting (the `liftZip2` scalar-replication arm IS
-- this constant `index`), and it keeps the 1c scalar model (bare element, "atoms
-- aren't special") while letting the scalar play uniformly in the representable
-- vocabulary — best-of-both vs. wrapping it as a rank-0 array value (rejected: it
-- fights atoms-aren't-special + the opaque-repr story). The 1c TYPE builder
-- `Array [] a = a` is UNCHANGED; only the operational `index` gains the constant arm.
--
-- The combinator library (reduce/scan/transpose) is Phase 3.
-- design/array-substrate.md §3/§4/§9.
Representable f = Representable index: f<a> -> i -> a tabulate: (i -> a) -> f<a>

-- Lift — the broadcasting / rank-polymorphism class (array-substrate Phase 2).
-- The SOUL of array languages, made dispatch: when a binop meets two arrays it
-- maps element-wise (`each = fmap`, already inherited from the `$map` Functor);
-- when it meets a SCALAR and an array it REPLICATES the scalar into the array's
-- frame (broadcasting). design/array-substrate.md §2c: broadcasting is NOT
-- subtyping — a scalar (rank-0, `Array [] a = a`, a bare element) and a vector
-- (rank-≥1, `{$tag:"Vec"}`) are a KIND difference; the rank-extend lives in the
-- `Lift`/`Rank` arm, "exactly the applicative-lifting discipline".
--
-- ONE method, the genuinely-new one:
--   • `zip2 : (a -> b -> c) -> f<a> -> f<b> -> f<c>` — lift a binop over two
--     arguments, broadcasting a rank-0 operand into the other's frame. The class
--     param `f` heads arg position 1 (after the binop at 0 — the `Traversable.
--     traverse` shape), so the dispatch seed is DERIVED from this `.fenel` decl
--     (system-entities.ts adds "Lift"→"f" to the loop; NO TS seed literal — the
--     Foldable/Applicative/Representable precedent). The `Lift (Map k)` instance
--     points `zip2` at `algebra:liftZip2`, a RANK-POLYMORPHIC driver: it routes
--     on the rank-0-vs-≥1 KIND test (`liftIsScalar` — a scalar has NO data
--     entries; §2c's kind difference, NOT a hand-rolled shape-equality `if`,
--     which is the Phase-4 conformance fence) and RECURSES, so a nested `Array`
--     broadcasts through every rank. The Phase-2 SURPRISE: `zip2` cannot be pure
--     arg-1 dispatch (a scalar-first `zip2 f 5 vec` has no `Lift` instance at arg
--     1), so the driver is a std rank-polymorphic function — the kind test
--     SELECTS the rank arm, which IS "instance resolution" in spirit, realized as
--     a driver rather than a class-method dispatch.
--
-- `each` (the APL map adverb `¨`) is NOT a `Lift` method — it IS `fmap`
-- (`each g xs = Functor.fmap g xs`), so it ships as a pure derived std alias
-- (`algebra:each`, algebra-methods.fenel), NOT duplicated as a class method (the
-- `toList`/`sequenceA` derived-std-fn precedent; D3). Same-shape `zip2` over
-- two DIFFERENT-length rank-≥1 arrays is the CONFORMANCE question = Phase 4
-- (key-EQUALITY refinement of `ssMap`, §2b) — NOT handled here (Phase 2 assumes
-- conformant shapes, the `vecIndex` in-domain-assumption precedent). `reduce`/
-- `scan`/`transpose` (the combinator library) = Phase 3.
Lift f = Lift zip2: (a -> b -> c) -> f<a> -> f<b> -> f<c>

-- Sequential — position-based slicing of a sequence (resolution-substrate
-- Phase 2, the FIRST lawful instance over the Phase-1 bare-name resolution
-- substrate). `take`/`drop`/`splitAt` cut a prefix/suffix of a sequence `f<a>`
-- by an index `n`. The class param `f` heads the SECOND argument (`f<a>`; the
-- count `n : Number` is arg 0), so dispatch keys on arg position 1 — DERIVED
-- from this `.fenel` decl by `dispatchPositionForSeed` (the `Traversable.
-- traverse` / `Lift.zip2` arg-1 shape; NO TS dispatch-seed literal — Sequential
-- lives in algebra.fenel, so `buildRegistry`'s data-driven algebra loop registers
-- it with ZERO new TS). Three methods, ALL argument-position + dispatchable:
--   • `take    : Number -> f<a> -> f<a>` — the first `n` elements.
--   • `drop    : Number -> f<a> -> f<a>` — all but the first `n`.
--   • `splitAt : Number -> f<a> -> List (f<a>)` — the prefix + the rest, as a
--     2-element list `[take n xs, drop n xs]` (positions 0 / 1). The CONCEPTUAL
--     type is the pair `(f<a>, f<a>)`, but tuple-VALUE literals `(a, b)` are a
--     current Fenel-parser gap (it parses tuple TYPES, not VALUES — and the
--     runtime has no `tuple` construction op either), so the pair is carried as a
--     2-element list until the tuple-value surface lands (parser-substrate). The
--     positional list is the faithful analog: index 0 = the take-part, 1 = the
--     drop-part, exactly the tuple's `_0` / `_1`.
--
-- D4 (Phase 0 lock): Sequential is MINIMAL. `length` / `null` come from
-- `Foldable` (`algebra:foldableLength`) — NOT duplicated here. `reverse` is a
-- separate `Reversible` concern — KEPT OUT. NO hard `Foldable` super (a `Vec`
-- knows its length without `foldr`); the length LAW references `foldableLength`,
-- so the law wants Foldable in scope, but the TYPE needn't require it.
--
-- THE LAWS (D4) are ordinary `Verified Boolean` record FIELDS — proof-as-
-- inhabitant dependent obligations ([[feedback_laws_supers_are_el_dependent_typing]]),
-- recognized structurally by `isLawField` and ERASED from the dispatch method
-- set by `seedFromType` (the `Labelable.labelNonEmpty` / `Iso.*Roundtrip`
-- precedent). Authored on the CLASS, OMITTED on the instances. The equations:
--   • seqTakeDropAppend : take n xs ++ drop n xs == xs        (the split is total)
--   • seqSplitAtTakeDrop: splitAt n xs == [take n xs, drop n xs]  (list carrier)
--   • seqTakeZeroEmpty  : take 0 xs == empty                  (Foldable's empty)
--   • seqDropZeroId     : drop 0 xs == xs
--   • seqTakeLength     : foldableLength (take n xs) == min n (foldableLength xs)
-- (needs `Foldable` — hence the soft, in-scope-only dependency above).
--
-- The List + Vec instances (algebra-instances.fenel) make `take`/`drop`/`splitAt`
-- resolve bare by the dispatch arg's type — `Sequential List` keeps the native
-- `std:take`/`std:drop` perf branch ([[feedback_opaque_is_optimization]]),
-- `Sequential Vec` is the leading-axis transforms (`algebra:take`/`algebra:drop`,
-- renamed from `takeAxis`/`dropAxis` in Phase 2 Commit 4 to force the codeName
-- collision that makes bare take/drop dispatch). This is the true
-- one-`take`-for-List+Vec subsumption (design/resolution-substrate.md).
Sequential f = Sequential take: Number -> f<a> -> f<a> drop: Number -> f<a> -> f<a> splitAt: Number -> f<a> -> List (f<a>) seqTakeDropAppend: Verified Boolean seqSplitAtTakeDrop: Verified Boolean seqTakeZeroEmpty: Verified Boolean seqDropZeroId: Verified Boolean seqTakeLength: Verified Boolean

-- Containable — value-level membership in a container (resolution-substrate Phase
-- 1b, retiring the `contains` grandfathered collision). `contains c x` asks "is
-- the element `x` in the container `c`?". The class param `c` heads the FIRST
-- argument (the container; the element `a` is arg 1), so dispatch keys on arg
-- position 0 — DERIVED from this decl by `dispatchPositionForSeed` (the bare
-- type-var-at-arg-0 shape, exactly `Eq.eq : a -> a -> Boolean`; NO TS
-- dispatch-seed literal — Containable lives in algebra.fenel, so buildRegistry's
-- data-driven algebra loop registers it with ZERO new TS, the `Sequential`
-- precedent). One method, argument-position + dispatchable:
--   • `contains : c -> a -> Boolean` — True when `a` is a member of `c`.
--
-- The String + List instances (algebra-instances.fenel) make bare `contains`
-- resolve by the container's reflected type — `Containable String` is the
-- substring test (`std:contains`), `Containable List` is element membership
-- (`std:includes`). Both KEEP their native bodies (the `listSequential` perf-branch
-- precedent — the instances POINT at the existing natives). This RETIRES the
-- last-wins footgun where bare `contains` silently resolved to `set:contains`
-- (the SetExpr decider) regardless of the actual container.
--
-- NOT a Containable instance: `set:contains` (`contains : SetExpr -> Any -> Maybe
-- Boolean`, the `DecidableMembership` SetExpr decider) is a DIFFERENT concept — it
-- returns `Maybe Boolean` (tri-state decidability), dispatches on SetExpr SHAPE,
-- and is invoked by fnId (the membership LAWS reference `{call:["set:contains",…]}`).
-- It STAYS separate, reachable by fnId — exactly as `DecidableMembership.contains`
-- stays reachable qualified. Its `contains` codeName is the SECOND binding that
-- makes the name multi-binding so the classifier routes it through `Containable`;
-- the dispatch targets are the String/List instances, never set:contains.
Containable c = Containable contains: c -> a -> Boolean
